ALS-DOF FAILURE AUDIT
======================

Timestamp: 2026-09-17
Puzzle: .1..3.2.6.....4...832561794.7.6458..6..9.7..5..5.1..6774...61.8...4...7...817..4.

This audit was run against the repository source. The earlier verifier-only
audit was insufficient because it verified only records already accepted by
the finder, and its expected-elimination calculation reused the finder rules.
This report separately checks the unique DLX solution and recomputes RCC
relations from the candidate grid.

BEFORE CORRECTION
-----------------
ALS records used: 748
Returned results: 3136 (hub: 32, DDS: 3104)
Unique DLX solution: 1
Returned eliminations that matched the actual solution: 2595
  DDS / S locked: 1730
  DDS / A intersection S: 865

Representative illegal result 1:
  Hub ALS {45789} r1c14
  Auxiliary ALS {123459} r2c1,r4c1,r6c1,r8c1,r9c1
  Auxiliary ALS {13479} r1c3,r4c3,r5c3,r7c3
  Auxiliary ALS {238} r6c4,r7c4
  Reported: r2c4<>8
  Actual solution: r2c4=8
  Cause: the code treated the shared 8 between the hub and ALS {238} as
  an eliminable RCC digit. A single RCC supplies a restriction; it does not
  eliminate itself. This pair also has no second shared digit for ALS-XZ.

Representative illegal result 2:
  Hub ALS {45789} r1c14
  Auxiliary ALS {589} r1c6,r1c8
  Auxiliary ALS {23489} r6c1,r6c2,r6c4,r6c6
  Auxiliary ALS {13479} r1c3,r4c3,r5c3,r7c3
  Reported: r5c3<>1
  Actual solution: r5c3=1
  Cause: the code treated digit 1 as locked to an auxiliary merely because
  it occurred in only one selected auxiliary. That ownership alone does not
  force an outside elimination.

The RCC finder also accepted shared digits occurring in multiple cells on
one or both ALSs. That is not a standard single-candidate RCC.

CORRECTION APPLIED
------------------
1. A shared digit is accepted as an RCC only when it occurs exactly once in
   each ALS and those two cells see each other.
2. DDS no longer eliminates the RCC digit itself.
3. DDS no longer treats auxiliary-only digits as locked solely by ownership.
4. Internal ALS-XZ records remain available when they provide their own
   restricted digit, second shared digit, and peer witness.

