WHAT THE FAILED CASES MEAN
--------------------------
These are search candidates, not proof failures:

* Insufficient candidates: the hub DOF could not be supplied by enough
  disjoint auxiliary ALSs.
* Duplicate RCC: two selected auxiliaries used the same restricted digit.
* Overlap: selected ALS cell sets were not disjoint.
* Incomplete coverage: the auxiliary RCC union did not cover every hub digit.
* No common-peer elimination: the structure passed its shape filters but
  no candidate saw every required source occurrence.
* Complete DDS with no internal ALS-XZ: the net had RCC coverage but no
  separately justified elimination survived.

POSTED r8c1<>9 LINE
-------------------
The exact four-ALS structure was not present in the untouched candidate
snapshot, and after the correction r8c1<>9 is not returned from that initial
snapshot. The posted line therefore needs the candidate grid from the step
where it was displayed before it can be validated exactly. Its absence here
does not prove the later-state line is invalid.

CONCLUSION
----------
The old DDS output was not logically safe. The verifier was blocking malformed
records only after the finder had already encoded the same incorrect rules.
The corrected run finds no solution-killing elimination on this puzzle, but
this is an instance-level falsification audit, not a general proof of every
higher-DOF ALS theorem.
